SDK patch 0320 (Max Payne 3 port): x360_edram_8bits_a_flotante, DEFAULT FALSE. What an 8-bit color view (EDRAM
format 0) drew is carried, when its pass closes and claims its rows, into the 7e3 views (f3/f12) of the same base,
pitch and MSAA as VALUES: a Copiar3D draw converts RGBA8 -> RGBA16F, and those views' tiles are marked seen exactly
as a transfer would. Root cause it closes (PORTING.md, runs 23-35 item 3): the tonemap draws the finished 0..1
picture into base 198's f0 view; the frame's last passes and the final copy the Swap fetches read the f12 view of
the same tiles, which keeps the raw lit HDR scene. The machinery's own carry never reaches it as values:
- EmpezarPase skips EdramPreparar for a pass whose first draw paints its color targets whole
  (dibujo_opaco_completo_, x360_edram_saltar_muertas, dibujos.cpp) - and the pass that follows the tonemap
  on those tiles is exactly such a whole-target opaque quad - so no transfer-in happens, and that pass's own claim
  (EdramCerrarPase -> EdramReclamar) then buries the f0 write under the f12 view's stale content;
- even when a prepare does run, EdramTransferir reinterprets the 8-bit BITS as 7e3 (kFsEdramColor's
  Empaquetar/Desempaquetar), not the picture's values.

The first console run (e725970, b.log, cvar on, NO gate) settled three things. (1) The carry works: the 300-frame
line counted 4.6-12.0 carries per frame in gameplay and some scenes rendered with right colors - when the tonemap
was the last 8-bit writer before the final copy. (2) The count was never stuck at 0: the windows that read
"0.0 de 8 bits a flotante" (and 0.0 transferencias, 0.00 Mpixeles - nothing claimed EDRAM rows at all) are Bink
movies and loading screens. (3) The ungated carry is the black screen: EVERY 8-bit pass on the base (composites,
clears feeding back) carried too, about 12 a frame, and their stale or cleared rows buried the lit scene under
black in most frames.

Amendments folded into this version of the patch:
- THE GATE (edram_pase_post_): only a pass that drew with one of the frame's post shaders (the tonemaps
  6671591252A5BCE2 / FE226603F40E2533 / CD9469857A0C532A and the FXAA 8D42760B0383C0D8) carries its 8-bit views'
  rows; composites and clears do not. The flag is set AFTER EmpezarPase returns, not before the call: EmpezarPase
  first closes the previous pass, and that close consumes and resets a flag set before it - the tonemap's own
  carry would never fire and the pass before each post pass would carry in its stead. Expected: about one carry
  a frame in gameplay (the tonemap into base 198's f3/f12 image) instead of 12.
- The destination lookup stays at edram_formato == 3 and that is complete: a 12 bind
  (k_2_10_10_10_FLOAT_AS_16_16_16_16, what the final copy reads) never reaches destinos_ as 12 - DestinoColor
  folds it into the f3 image of the same key (destinos.cpp) and EdramVista's FormatoBase(12) is 3. f3 and f12
  are ONE image (run 65's ten f12 draws land in it); the b.log counts prove the lookup found it.
- LOGGING: the per-carry line prints once per 10 s outside a dump frame (not only under EdramRegistrando), and a
  failed carry says why, once per 10 s: no 7e3 view of that base/pitch/msaa, or Copiar3D refused. One run then
  shows the carry per tonemap AND the reason if it still fails.

Off by default: this is architecture-level and must not change behavior until the console test enables it via
mp3.toml (x360_edram_8bits_a_flotante = true); a failed Copiar3D falls back to the old transfer path (the tiles
keep their claim).

Amendment v3 (e25326c) and v4 (f399cd), now folded into this patch file: the carry AT THE CLOSE was undone by the
game's later passes on the same tiles (v2's bullet time), so v4 snapshots the post pass's 8-bit output at its close
into a side image (edram_posteado_, nothing later draws on it) and the frontal copy INSTALLS it into the 7e3 view
right before the resolve (destinos.cpp Copiar). Menu and cutscene rendered right; gameplay stayed black - and v4
had no logging of its own.

Amendment v5 (this version), from v4.log (f399cd) and the mp3_030 frame trace:
- BOTH links fire in gameplay: the 300-frame [edram] counter read 0.5-1.0 "de 8 bits a flotante" in every gameplay
  window, and in v4 the ONLY increment left is the install's Copiar3D (destinos.cpp), which cannot run unless a
  snapshot succeeded (edram_posteado_valido_). The machinery is innocent; what it installs is black.
- WHY: the tonemap and the frame's LATER 8-bit writers share ONE renderer pass. mp3_030's frame (Swap 3652): the
  tonemap quad (draw 2285, vs 1924 ps 3237) into c198/f0, then 24 skipped prim-1 probes, then TWENTY draws of the
  composite (vs 573 ps 1895: ten color-masked, ten blending color, full-screen scissor) into the SAME c198/f0 -
  identical claves (base, format, pitch, MSAA), so the pass only closes at the f0 resolve (0654D000), AFTER the
  composite. v4's close-snapshot captured what the composite left. v1's frames whose LAST 8-bit writer was the
  tonemap (the composite wrote nothing) showed the correct colors: the tonemap's own output is the picture, and
  the composite's color rounds are the blackener. The FXAA reads 0654D000 right after (v4.log: [exposicion] fxaa
  and "PS n2262 slot 0: base 0654D000" at the same timestamp), which spread the blackened resolve in v1's bad
  frames.
- THE FIX: Dibujar snapshots right after EACH post draw into an 8-bit target (EdramSnapshotPost, the same copy
  the close made), then invalidates the pass binding (g_.pase_valido, as EdramEstencilPendiente does) and consumes
  edram_pase_post_ so the composite's close cannot snapshot over it. The close path keeps the snapshot as a
  fallback (a draw rejected between bookkeeping and submission). The install is unchanged - it fired 1.0/frame.
- INSTRUMENTATION: one line per 10 s names the snapshot's source image (Vista: base, format, pitch, serie) and
  counts the color images of that base in destinos_ (DestinoColor keys by base, format, pitch, MSAA), and three
  texel probes of the source and of the snapshot print at the next Swap ([edram-diag] texel, half-float values).
  One run settles it: values like 0.1-0.9 in the probes and correct colors on screen close the saga; all-zero
  probes exonerate the composite and put the black inside the tonemap's own draw.


Amendment v6 (the combination, from v5's console post-mortem): v4's firing wiring with v5's snapshot moment.
- v5 (ed100d0) fired NOTHING on console (no "[edram] snapshot post" line, the install counter 0.0 in active
  windows, the raw-white picture back) and the log rules out every explanation but a silent death inside
  EdramSnapshotPost: the huella detection matched live ([verts-tonemap] lines inside the active windows - the
  constants are XXH3 of the guest container bytes, they cannot drift with the host library), the tonemap's draws
  were submitted ("0 rechazados"), the EDRAM machinery ran (5.1-6.7 transfers a frame in those windows), the cvar
  was on (the day's toml), and the NRO carries the v5 strings (a fresh build). v5's EdramSnapshotPost could return
  false at three points without a trace (ancho/filas 0, the side image not created, Copiar3D refused) - none of
  them logged.
- THE COMBINATION: the after-draw snapshot (dibujos.cpp) now also requires edram_pase_post_ - the PASS flag v4's
  run demonstrably engaged (its close-time snapshot fed the 1.0/frame install) - beside the draw's own huella; the
  close keeps the snapshot as v4's fallback and REGAINS its flag reset at the end of EdramCerrarPase (v5 had
  dropped it, leaving the gate stale-true across unrelated closes); the install is untouched (destinos.cpp).
- THE SIDE IMAGE IS RGBA8 now (it was RGBA16F): the install's Copiar3D makes the 8-bit -> float conversion - v1's
  proven direction (its carries rendered correct colors) - and a raw copy-engine copy (dkCmdBufCopyImage, both
  sides RGBA8, RestaurarContenido's own fallback pattern) takes the snapshot when Copiar3D refuses, removing the
  one silent failure class left. A snapshot that still cannot be taken says why, once per 10 s.
- THE LINE: "[edram] snapshot post <Vista> ... tras el dibujo|cierre, version N: ..." once per 10 s names the path
  that took it; "tras el dibujo" in a gameplay window is the proof the after-draw wiring fires. The texel probes
  stay (they read guest memory: zeros are normal there; the bindings are their value).

